# XSH pthread_attr_getschedparam(), pthread_attr_setschedparam()
# Copyright (c) 2026 Nicholas Martin. All rights reserved.
#
# Which priorities are valid for the default policy is not specified. The
# SCHED_SPORADIC members need the TSP option and are not tested. How the
# stored parameters apply to a created thread is tested with
# xsh/pthread_attr_getinheritsched.

assertion  XSH.pthread_attr_getschedparam.001
anchor     functions/pthread_attr_getschedparam.html#DESCRIPTION
anchor     functions/pthread_attr_getschedparam.html#RETURN VALUE
class      required
since      2008
says       On a newly initialized attributes object,
           pthread_attr_getschedparam() returns 0; storing the result
           back with pthread_attr_setschedparam() returns 0, and
           pthread_attr_getschedparam() then returns 0 and stores the
           same sched_priority.
note       EINVAL or ENOTSUP from the set is a may-fail or unsupported
           case and leaves the test UNRESOLVED.
test       t/001-default-round-trip.c

assertion  XSH.pthread_attr_getschedparam.002
anchor     functions/pthread_attr_getschedparam.html#DESCRIPTION
anchor     functions/pthread_attr_getschedparam.html#RETURN VALUE
class      conditional
when       TPS
since      2001
says       With the policy of the attributes object set to SCHED_FIFO,
           pthread_attr_setschedparam() with the lowest and then the
           highest SCHED_FIFO priority returns 0, and
           pthread_attr_getschedparam() returns 0 and stores that
           sched_priority each time.
note       Priorities in the policy's range are valid; a refusal
           (EINVAL, ENOTSUP, EPERM) leaves the test UNRESOLVED.
test       t/002-fifo-range.c
